To configure SSL for a mail server, you need to secure the communication 
between mail clients (like Outlook or Thunderbird) and the mail server
 (like Postfix, Exim, Dovecot, etc.). Here's a clear, step-by-step guide
for a typical Linux-based server (like one using Postfix + Dovecot) and works similarly for cPanel/WHM setups.

🔒 Steps to Configure SSL for Mail Server
✅ Prerequisites:
A valid domain name (e.g., mail.example.com)
A valid SSL certificate for that domain (can use Let’s Encrypt or paid SSL)
A running Mail Transfer Agent (MTA) like Postfix or Exim
A Mail Delivery Agent (MDA) like Dovecot
Port 25, 465, 587 (SMTP), and 993/995 (IMAP/POP) open

1. Install SSL Certificate
If using Let’s Encrypt, run:
sudo apt install certbot
sudo certbot certonly --standalone -d mail.example.com

Your SSL certificate and key will be saved in:
/etc/letsencrypt/live/mail.example.com/fullchain.pem

2. Configure SSL in Postfix (SMTP Server)
Edit Postfix config:
sudo nano /etc/postfix/main.cf

Add or update the following lines:
smtpd_tls_cert_file=/etc/letsencrypt/live/mail.example.com/fullchain.pem
smtpd_tls_key_file=/etc/letsencrypt/live/mail.example.com/privkey.pem
smtpd_use_tls=yes
smtpd_tls_session_cache_database = btree:${data_directory}/smtpd_scache
smtp_tls_security_level = may
smtpd_tls_security_level = may
smtpd_tls_auth_only = yes


3. Configure SSL in pop server/imap [dovecot]
sudo nano /etc/dovecot/conf.d/10-ssl.conf
sudo systemctl restart dovecot   - reboot the server

4. Open Mail Ports in Firewall
sudo ufw allow 465,587,993,995/tcp
sudo ufw reload

5. Test Your Configuration
Use an email client like Thunderbird or Outlook and configure it with:
Incoming (IMAP/POP): mail.example.com port 993/995 (SSL)
Outgoing (SMTP): mail.example.com port 465 or 587 (SSL/TLS)

6. (Optional) WHM/cPanel Configuration
If you use cPanel/WHM, SSL for mail is typically handled automatically:
Go to WHM > Manage Service SSL Certificates
Install or renew SSL for:
Exim (SMTP)
Dovecot (IMAP/POP3)
You can use AutoSSL to automatically apply SSL to mail services too